# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file, line endings are decided by whatever core.autocrlf happens
# to be set to on each contributor's machine. On Windows that setting rewrites
# every text file to CRLF at checkout, which broke 29 visualisation tests: the
# renderers emit \n, the golden files they are compared against arrived on disk
# with \r\n, and every byte-for-byte comparison failed. The files in the
# repository were correct the whole time — they were being modified between
# checkout and test.
#
# The policy is LF everywhere, in the repository and in the working tree, on
# every platform. That is already true of all 422 tracked files, so this file
# changes no content; it only stops the working tree from drifting.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Golden fixtures are compared byte for byte, so they are the files with the
# least tolerance for any rewriting. Called out explicitly rather than left to
# the rule above: it states the requirement at the place it matters, and keeps
# them protected if the global rule is ever loosened. `text` still normalises
# to LF on commit, so a fixture regenerated on any platform stays correct.
tests/golden/** text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF regardless of any future change above: a CRLF
# script fails at its shebang with a confusing message about a missing
# interpreter.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The repository currently contains no binary files. These rules exist so the
# first one added — a diagram in the docs, a fixture image — is not corrupted
# by the text handling above.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
